.TH killall 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
killall \- signal every process on the machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B killall
[\fIsignal\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B killall
sends a signal to
.I every
process on the system except
.BR init ,
itself, and its own parent.  Without an argument it sends signal 15,
.BR SIGTERM .
.SH NOTES
Read that again before using it.  This is not the Linux
.BR killall .
It takes no process name, and it does not stop at processes you own or
at your own terminal: it is closer to the System V program of the same
name, used by
.B /etc/rc.halt
when the machine is being shut down.
.PP
.B killall
run from a shell will kill the other logins, background jobs,
.BR httpd ,
anything playing a sound, and whatever else is running.  Your own shell
survives only because it is the parent.
.PP
To signal one program by name there is no ready-made command:
.BR ps (1)
to find the pid,
.BR kill (1)
to signal it.
.SH THE SIGNAL
A
.I number
only.  Names are not accepted here, unlike in
.BR kill (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The number of processes signalled, or 255 if
.B /dev/proc
could not be read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kill (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR shutdown (1)
